(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_biodiversityandconservation=[{id:"neet-2026-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Causes of biodiversity loss",q:'"The Evil Quartet" of biodiversity loss includes which of the following?',o:["Over-exploitation; Alien species invasions; Air pollution; Co-extinctions","Habitat loss and fragmentation; over-exploitation; Alien species invasions; Co-extinctions","Habitat loss and fragmentation; Air pollution; Water pollution; Co-extinctions","Over-exploitation; Alien species invasions; Soil pollution; Co-extinctions"],c:1,s:`'The Evil Quartet' is the nickname for the four major causes of biodiversity loss.
They are habitat loss and fragmentation, over-exploitation, alien species invasions and co-extinctions.`},{id:"neet-2026-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Causes of biodiversity loss",q:`Which of the following statements are correct?
A. The Amazon rainforest being cut and cleared for cultivation of soyabeans is an example of habitat loss.
B. Steller's sea cow and passenger pigeon became extinct due to over-exploitation by humans.
C. The Nile perch introduced into Lake Victoria in East Africa helped in population growth of cichlid fish in the lake.
D. Water hyacinth is an invasive species.
E. When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species associated with it are not affected.`,o:["B, C and D only","A, B and D only","A, B and E only","C, D and E only"],c:1,s:`The Nile perch introduced into Lake Victoria led to extinction of more than 200 species of cichlid fish, so C is wrong.
When a species becomes extinct, the plant and animal species linked to it in an obligatory way also become extinct, so E is wrong.
Statements A, B and D are correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Bioprospecting",q:"Exploring molecular, genetic and species-level diversity for products of economic importance is called",o:["Biomagnification","Biofortification","Bioremediation","Bioprospecting"],c:3,s:"Exploring molecular, genetic and species-level diversity for products of economic importance is called bioprospecting."},{id:"neet-2026-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"In situ conservation",q:"Which of the following is an in situ conservation method?",o:["Sacred Groves","Wildlife Safari Parks","Botanical Gardens","Seed Banks"],c:0,s:`In-situ conservation is exemplified by sacred groves.
Wildlife safari parks, botanical gardens and seed banks are examples of ex-situ conservation.
Note: Printed solution text says safari parks, botanical gardens, seed banks are in-situ; this is a slip, they are ex-situ. Printed answer 1 kept.`},{id:"neet-2026-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Mass extinctions",q:"Since the origin and diversification of life on Earth, there have been five episodes of mass extinction of species. How is the sixth extinction, which is in progress, different from the previous episodes?",o:["The current species extinction rates are far lower than those in previous episodes.","The present species extinction rates are 100 to 1000 times faster than in the pre-human times.","The present net species extinction rate is zero.","The current species extinction rate is nearly 10 times faster than previous episodes."],c:1,s:`The current, sixth episode of mass extinction is estimated to be 100 to 1000 times faster than in pre-human times.
Our activities are responsible for these faster rates.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q147",pn:147,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Species diversity patterns",q:`Arrange the following in descending order of number of species in the Amazonian rain forest.
(a) Plants
(b) Birds
(c) Fishes
(d) Invertebrates
(e) Mammals
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(d) > (a) > (c) > (b) > (e)","(e) > (b) > (a) > (c) > (d)","(b) > (a) > (d) > (c) > (e)","(c) > (b) > (d) > (e) > (a)"],c:0,s:`The Amazonian rain forest has more than 40,000 species of plants.
It has 3,000 species of fishes, 1300 of birds, 427 of mammals and 378 of reptiles.
It has more than 1,25,000 invertebrates.
So the decreasing order is (d) > (a) > (c) > (b) > (e).`},{id:"neet-2025-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Ex-situ conservation",q:"Which one of the following is an example of ex-situ conservation?",o:["National Park","Wildlife Sanctuary","Zoos and botanical gardens","Protected areas"],c:2,s:`Zoological parks (zoos), botanical gardens and wildlife safari parks are examples of ex-situ conservation.
Sacred groves, biosphere reserves, national parks and wildlife sanctuaries are examples of in-situ conservation.`},{id:"neet-2025-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Conservation terms",q:`Match List I with List II:
List I \u2014 List II
A. The Evil Quartet \u2014 I. Cryopreservation
B. Ex situ conservation \u2014 II. Alien species invasion
C. Lantana camara \u2014 III. Causes of biodiversity losses
D. Dodo \u2014 IV. Extinction
Choose the option with all correct matches.`,o:["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],c:1,s:`The Evil Quartet - causes of biodiversity losses.
Ex situ conservation - cryopreservation.
Lantana camara - alien species invasion.
Dodo - extinction.`},{id:"neet-2024-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Ex-situ conservation",q:"The type of conservation in which the treatened species are taken out from their naturaly habitat and placed in special setting where they can be protected and given special care is called",o:["Semi-conservative method","Sustainable development","in-situ conservation","Biodiversity conservation"],c:3,s:`Taking threatened species out of their natural habitat and placing them in special settings for protection and care is ex-situ conservation.
Ex-situ conservation is a type of biodiversity conservation.
So the printed answer is option (4).`},{id:"neet-2024-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Species richness in tropics",q:`Tropical regions show greatest level of species richness because
(A) Tropical latitudes have remained relatively undisturbed for milions of years, hence more time was available for species diversification.
(B) Tropical environments are more seasonal
(C) More solar energy is available in tropics
(D) Constant environments promote niche specialization.
(E) Tropical environments are constant and predictable.
Choose the correct answer form the options given below :`,o:["A, B and E only","A, B and D only","A, C, D and E only","A and B only"],c:2,s:`Only statement B is wrong.
Unlike temperate regions, tropical environments are less seasonal, fairly constant and predictable.
So A, C, D and E are correct.`},{id:"neet-2024-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Causes of biodiversity loss",q:`These are regarded as major causes of biodiversity loss :
(A) Over exploitation  (B) Co-extinction  (C) Mutation
(D) Habitat loss and fragmentation  (E) Migration
Choose the correct option :`,o:["A, B and E only","A, B and D only","A, C and D only","A, B, C and D only"],c:1,s:`The major causes of biodiversity loss are:
(1) alien species invasions
(2) co-extinctions
(3) habitat loss and fragmentation
(4) over-exploitation.
Mutation and migration are not on this list.
So the correct option is A, B and D only.`},{id:"neet-2024-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Conservation organisations",q:"List of endangered species was released by-:",o:["Foam","IUCN","GEAC","WWF"],c:1,s:"The list of endangered species is released by IUCN."},{id:"neet-2024-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Biodiversity scientists",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Robert May \u2014 I. Species area relationship
B. Alexander von Humboldt \u2014 II. Long term ecosystem experiment using out door plots
C. Paul Ehrlich \u2014 III. Global species diversity at about 7 million
D. David Tilman \u2014 IV. Rivet popper hypothesis
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],c:3,s:`Robert May put global species diversity at about 7 million.
Alexander von Humboldt described the species-area relationship.
Paul Ehrlich used the rivet popper analogy to show the role of species in an ecosystem.
David Tilman did long term ecosystem experiments using outdoor plots.
So A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2023-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Earth Summit and conservation",q:`The historic Convention on Biological Diversity. "The Earth Summit' was held in Rio de Janeiro in the year :`,o:["2002","1985","1992","1986"],c:2,s:`The Convention on Biological Diversity, the Earth Summit, was held in Rio de Janeiro in 1992.
It asked all nations to take steps to conserve biodiversity and use its benefits sustainably.`},{id:"neet-2023-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"The Evil Quartet",q:"Among \u2018The Evil Quartet\u2019, which one is considered the most important cause driving extinction of species?",o:["Co-extinctions","Habitat loss and fragmentation","Over exploitation for economic gain","Alien species invasions"],c:1,s:"Habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important cause driving plants and animals to extinction."},{id:"neet-2022-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Ex situ conservation",q:"Which of the following is not a method of ex situ conservation?",o:["National Parks","Micropropagation","Cryopreservation","In vitro fertilization"],c:0,s:`In-situ conservation protects the whole ecosystem on site.
National parks are in-situ conservation.
Micropropagation, cryopreservation and in-vitro fertilisation are ex-situ methods.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q104",pn:104,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Biodiversity patterns Amazon",q:`Give the correct descending order of organisms with reference to their estimated number found in Amazon forest.
(a) Plants (b) Invertebrates (c) Fishes (d) Mammals (e) Birds
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["(b) > (a) > (c) > (e) > (d)","(a) > (b) > (e) > (d) > (c)","(a) > (c) > (d) > (b) > (e)","(b) > (a) > (e) > (d) > (c)"],c:0,s:`Approximate species counts in the Amazon:
Insects (invertebrates) 2.5 million.
Plants 50,000.
Fishes 3000.
Birds 1300.
Mammals 430.
Arranged in descending order this gives option 1.`},{id:"neet-2022-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Causes of biodiversity loss",q:"Habitat loss and fragmentation, over exploitation, alien species invasion and co-extinction are causes for:",o:["Competition","Biodiversity loss","Natality","Population explosion"],c:1,s:"These four are the main causes of biodiversity loss."},{id:"neet-2022r-q120",pn:120,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Johannesburg summit 2002",q:"The World Summit on sustainable development held in 2002 in Johannesburg, South Africa pledged for :",o:["Collection and preservation of seeds of different genetic strains of commercially important plants.","A significant reduction in the current rate of biodiversity loss.","Declaration of more biodiversity hotspots.","Increase in agricultural production."],c:1,s:`At the 2002 Johannesburg summit, 190 countries pledged to achieve by 2010 a significant reduction in the rate of biodiversity loss.
This was at global, regional and local levels.`},{id:"neet-2022-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"CNG and air pollution",q:"The entire fleet of buses in Delhi were converted to CNG from diesel. In reference to this, which one of the following statements is false?",o:["The same diesel engine is used in CNG buses making the cost of conversion low","It is cheaper than diesel","It cannot be adulterated like diesel","CNG burns more efficiently than diesel"],c:0,s:`CNG is cheaper than diesel and petrol.
It burns more efficiently.
It cannot be adulterated like diesel or petrol.
The same diesel engine cannot be used for CNG buses, so statement (1) is false.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q143",pn:143,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Conservation examples",q:`Match List - I with List - II:
List-I: (a) Sacred groves (b) Zoological park (c) Nile perch (d) Amazon forest
List-II: (i) Alien species (ii) Release of large quantity of oxygen (iii) Ex-situ conservation (iv) Khasi Hills in Meghalaya
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],c:1,s:`Sacred groves are forest patches protected for cultural beliefs, such as in the Khasi Hills of Meghalaya.
A zoological park keeps animals outside their habitat, which is ex-situ conservation.
Nile perch was introduced into Lake Victoria and wiped out over 200 native cichlid species, so it is an alien species.
The Amazon forest covers over 14% of the earth's land and gives out a large amount of oxygen.`},{id:"neet-2022-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"In-situ conservation",q:"In-situ conservation refers to:",o:["Conserve only high-risk species","Conserve only endangered species","Conserve only extinct species","Protect and conserve the whole ecosystem"],c:3,s:`In-situ (on-site) conservation protects the whole ecosystem.
This protects biodiversity at all levels.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q166",pn:166,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"CNG versus diesel",q:`Why CNG is considered better fuel than diesel?
      (a) It can not be adulterated.
      (b) It takes less time to fill the fuel tank.
      (c) It burns more efficiently.
      (d) It is cheaper.
      (e) It is less inflammable.
      Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["(c), (d), (e) only","(a), (b), (c), (e) only","(a), (c), (d) only","(a), (b), (d), (e) only"],c:2,s:`CNG cannot be adulterated.
It burns more efficiently.
It is cheaper.
(e) is wrong because CNG is less flammable, not 'less inflammable'.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q171",pn:171,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Endemism",q:"Western Ghats have a large number of plants and animal species that are not found anywhere else. Which of the following term is used to notify such species?",o:["Vulnerable species","Threatened species","Keystone species","Endemic species"],c:3,s:`Endemism means a taxon is limited to a small area and found nowhere else.
Such species are called endemic.`},{id:"neet-2022-q186",pn:186,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Air pollution control",q:"Given below are two statements: Statements I: In a scrubber the exhaust from the thermal plant is passed through the electric wires to charge the dust particles. Statement II: Particulate matter (PM 2.5) cannot be removed by scrubber but can be removed by an electrostatic precipitator. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the option given below:",o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are in correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:2,s:`A scrubber removes gases like SO2 by passing exhaust through a limestone slurry. It does not use electric wires, so Statement I is wrong.
The electrostatic precipitator charges dust particles with electric wires and removes about 99% of particulate matter, even PM 2.5.
So Statement II is correct.`},{id:"neet-2020-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Environmental protocols",q:"Montreal protocol was signed in 1987 for control of :",o:["Emission of ozone depleting substances","Release of Green House gases","Disposal of e-wastes","Transport of Genetically modified organisms from one country to another"],c:0,s:`The Montreal Protocol (1987) controls the emission of ozone depleting substances.
Note: The paper has no environmental issues chapter; nearest chapter used.`},{id:"neet-2020-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Ozone depletion",q:"Snow-blindness in Antarctic region is due to :",o:["Inflammation of cornea due to high dose of UV-B radiation","High reflection of light from snow","Damage to retina caused by infra-red rays","Freezing of fluids in the eye by low temperature"],c:0,s:`Ozone depletion lets more UV-B reach the earth.
UV-B inflames the cornea; in snow regions this is snow-blindness.
Note: The paper has no environmental issues chapter; nearest chapter used.`},{id:"neet-2020-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Patterns of diversity",q:"Which of the following regions of the globe exhibits highest species diversity?",o:["Madagascar","Himalayas","Amazon foresta","Western Ghats of India"],c:2,s:"The Amazon rainforest has the highest species diversity on the earth."},{id:"neet-2020-q086",pn:86,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Species diversity",q:"According to Robert May, the global species diversity is about :",o:["20 million","50 million","7 million","1.5 million"],c:2,s:"Robert May estimated global species diversity at about 7 million."},{id:"neet-2019-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Conservation efforts",q:"The Earth summit held in Rio de Janeiro in 1992 was called :",o:["to assess threat posed to native species by invasive weed species","for immediate steps to discontinue use of CFCs that were damaging the ozone layer","to reduce CO2 emissions and global warming","for conservation of biodiversity and sustainable utilization of its benefits"],c:3,s:`The 1992 Rio Earth Summit was a call to all nations.
Its aim was to conserve biodiversity and use its benefits sustainably.`},{id:"neet-2019-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Radioactive waste",q:"Which of these following methods is the most suitable for disposal of nuclear waste ?",o:["Dump the waste within rocks under deep ocean","Bury the waste within rocks deep below the Earth\u2019s surface","Shoot the waste into space","Bury the waste under Antarctic ice-cover"],c:1,s:`Nuclear waste is highly harmful and stays radioactive for a very long time.
It is stored in suitable containers and buried within rocks deep below the Earth\u2019s surface.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2019-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Causes of biodiversity loss",q:"Which of the following is the most important cause for animals and plants being driven to extinction?",o:["Economic exploitation","Alien species invasion","Habitat loss and fragmentation","Drought and floods"],c:2,s:`Habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important cause of extinction.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2019-q060",pn:60,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"International protocols",q:"Which of the following protocols did aim for reducing emission of chlorofluorocarbons into the atmosphere?",o:["Gothenburg Protocol","Geneva Protocol","Montreal Protocol","Kyoto Protocol"],c:2,s:`The Montreal Protocol (1987) was made to control the release of ozone-depleting substances such as CFCs.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2019-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"In situ conservation",q:"Which one of the following is not a method of in situ conservation of biodiversity?",o:["Botanical Garden","Sacred Grove","Biosphere Reserve","Wildife Sanctuary"],c:0,s:`In situ means conservation in the natural habitat.
Sacred groves, biosphere reserves and sanctuaries are in situ.
A botanical garden is ex situ.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2019-q090",pn:90,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Solid waste management",q:"Polyblend, a fine powder of recycled modified plastic, has proved to be a good material for :",o:["Construction of roads","Making tubes and pipes","Making plastic sacks","use as a fertilizer"],c:0,s:`Polyblend is mixed with bitumen to lay roads.
Such roads last longer and use up waste plastic.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2018-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"In-situ and ex-situ conservation",q:"All of the following are included in 'Ex-situ conservation' except -",o:["Wildlife safari parks","sacred groves","Botanical gardens","seed banks"],c:1,s:`Ex-situ conservation is outside the natural habitat: zoos, safari parks, botanical gardens and seed banks.
Sacred groves protect species in their natural habitat, so they are in-situ.`},{id:"neet-2017-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Biosphere reserves",q:"The region of Biosphere Reserve which is legally protected and where no human activity is allowed is known as :",o:["Restoration zone","Core zone","Buffer zone","Transition zone"],c:1,s:`A biosphere reserve has a core zone, a buffer zone and a transition zone.
The core zone is legally protected and undisturbed, with no human activity.`},{id:"neet-2017-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Ex-situ conservation",q:"Which one of the following is related to Ex-situ conservation of threatened animals and plants ?",o:["Himalayan region","Wildlife Safari Parks","Biodiversity hot spots","Amazon rainforest"],c:1,s:`Ex-situ conservation means protecting species outside their natural habitat, such as in zoos, botanical gardens and safari parks.
The Himalayas, hot spots and the Amazon rainforest are natural habitats (in-situ).
So wildlife safari parks is the answer.`},{id:"neet-2017-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Species-area relationship",q:"Alexander Von Humbolt described for the first time :",o:["Population Growth equation","Ecological Biodiversity","Laws of limiting factor","Species are relationships"],c:3,s:`Alexander von Humboldt observed that within a region, species richness increases with area.
This is the species-area relationship.`},{id:"neet-2017-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Air pollution: aerosols",q:"Which one of the following statements is not valid for aerosols ?",o:["They have negative impact on agricultural land","They are harmful to human health","They alter rainfall and monsoon patterns","They cause increased agricultural productivity"],c:3,s:`Aerosols are fine particles in the air that cause smog and haze.
They harm health, reduce sunlight and crop yield, and alter rain patterns.
Increased productivity is not valid.`},{id:"neet-2016-q051",pn:51,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Causes of extinction",q:"Which of the following is the most important cause of animals and plants being driven to extinction ?",o:["Alien species invasion","Habitat loss and fragmentation","Co-extinctions","Over-exploitation"],c:1,s:`Among the four causes of biodiversity loss (the 'evil quartet'), habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important.
It is the most common cause of species extinction across the globe.`},{id:"neet-2016-q130",pn:130,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Joint Forest Management",q:"Joint Forest Management Concept was introduced in India during :",o:["1970s","1980s","1990s","1960s"],c:1,s:"Joint Forest Management, in which local communities help protect forests, was introduced in India in the 1980s."},{id:"neet-2016b-q130",pn:130,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Biodiversity hotspots",q:"How many hot spots of biodiversity in the world have been identified till date by Norman Myers ?",o:["17","25","34","43"],c:2,s:`Norman Myers first identified 25 hotspots, later raised to 34 as more regions were added.
The NCERT-based answer is 34.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q134",pn:134,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Matching terms",q:"Which of the following is correctly matched ?",o:["Aerenchyma-Opuntia","Age pyramid-Biome","Parthenium hysterophorus-Threat to biodiversity","Stratification-Population"],c:2,s:`Aerenchyma is found in hydrophytes, but Opuntia is a xerophyte: wrong.
An age pyramid describes a population, not a biome: wrong.
Stratification is the vertical layering of a community, not a population: wrong.
Parthenium is an invasive weed and a threat to biodiversity: correct.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q135",pn:135,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Red Data Book",q:"Red List contains data or information on",o:["all economically important plants","plants whose products are in international trade","threatened species","marine vertebrates only"],c:2,s:`The IUCN Red List (Red Data Book) records the conservation status of species.
It lists species that are threatened with extinction, in categories such as critically endangered, endangered and vulnerable.
So it is threatened species.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q178",pn:178,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"National parks",q:"Which of the following National Parks is home to the famous musk deer or hangul ?",o:["Keibul Lamjao National Park, Manipur","Bandhavgarh National Park, Madhya Pradesh","Eaglenest Wildlife Sanctuary, Arunachal Pradesh","Dachigam National Park, Jammu & Kashmir"],c:3,s:`Dachigam National Park in Jammu and Kashmir is the home of the hangul (Kashmir stag).
Keibul Lamjao is known for the sangai deer, and Bandhavgarh for tigers.
So Dachigam.`}]})();
